# Shell scripts MUST keep LF endings, even when checked out on Windows.
#
# Without this, git's core.autocrlf=true rewrites every *.sh to CRLF on
# checkout. Linux containers then read the trailing \r as part of the last
# token on the line, and the script dies in ways that read as nonsense:
#
#   /init-topics.sh: line 2: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# That exact failure meant `redpanda-init` silently created no Kafka topics
# in the local stack, so every consumer had nothing to consume. It never
# reproduced in CI, because CI checks out on Linux and gets LF anyway —
# the whole class of bug is invisible to the pipeline and only ever bites
# a developer on Windows.
#
# Scoped deliberately to *.sh rather than a blanket `* text=auto eol=lf`:
# the goal is to protect files that a Linux shell executes, not to
# renormalise the entire tree.
*.sh text eol=lf
